<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian Social Union in Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CSU</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Christian Social Union in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> is a conservative political party founded in 1945 that operates only in Bavaria, where it has led the state government almost continuously since 1946. In federal politics it forms a joint parliamentary group with the Christian Democratic Union. Franz Josef Strauss was its dominant leader from 1961 to 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-social-union-in-bavaria.jpg" alt="Christian Social Union in Bavaria" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThecentreCZ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Christian_Social_Union_in_Bavaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Christian Social Union in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, founded in 1945, is regarded as the de facto successor of the Catholic Bavarian People's Party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> period. It is a Christian democratic and conservative party with a regionalist identity, and it operates only in Bavaria, while its larger counterpart, the Christian Democratic Union, operates in the other states. The CSU has led the Bavarian state government since 1946, except from 1954 to 1957, when the Social Democrat Wilhelm Hoegner formed a coalition government. The separatist Bavaria Party had at first competed for the same voters, since both saw themselves as successors to the Bavarian People's Party; the CSU won this contest, and in 1966 the Bavaria Party left the state parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Franz Josef Strauß left the strongest legacy. He led the party from 1961 until his death in 1988, held four federal ministerial posts between 1953 and 1969, and was Minister-President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1978. In 1980 he was the first CSU leader to stand for the chancellorship, but lost to the incumbent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-schmidt">Helmut Schmidt</a>. No chancellor has ever come from the CSU. Edmund Stoiber, leader from 1999, ran for chancellor in 2002 and also lost.</p><p>Before 2008 the CSU regularly won absolute majorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, a dominance unique among the German states. In 2003 it won 60.7 per cent of the vote and 124 of 180 seats, the first two-thirds majority in a German state parliament. It lost its majority in 2008, when its share fell to 43.4 per cent, and governed in coalition with the Free Democratic Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In the Bundestag the CSU forms a common group with the Christian Democratic Union, known as the Union. Below the federal level the two parties are entirely independent. The CSU and the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> did not sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basic-law-for-the-federal-republic-of-germany">Grundgesetz</a> of the Federal Republic, as they could not agree with the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> into two states after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The party is divided into ten district associations, 105 county associations and almost 3,000 local associations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christian Social Union in Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1945 Bavaria was in the American zone; its 1946 constitution created a state long dominated by the <b>Christian Social Union</b>, which built a high-technology economy.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-bavaria">Free State of Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
